  q(HCE,'Which form do employers use to verify a new hire’s identity and right to work in the U.S.?','Form I-9',['Form W-4','Form 1040','Form CMS-1500'],'D','Employers must keep I-9 forms and may face fines for missing ones.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'Which OSHA clause requires employers to keep the workplace free of known serious hazards?','General Duty Clause',['Hazard Communication','Bloodborne Pathogens','Lockout/Tagout'],'A','It applies even when no specific OSHA standard covers the hazard.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'An employee files a False Claims Act lawsuit on behalf of the government. What is this called?','A qui tam action',['A class action','A tort claim','A bench trial'],'B','Whistleblowers may receive a share of the money recovered and are protected from retaliation.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'Why do many hospitals test employees for drugs?','To protect patient and staff safety',['To set employee pay rates','To pick vacation dates','To check each applicant’s job references'],'C','Policies must follow state laws and protect the privacy of results.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'Under employment-at-will, an employer may usually:','End employment for any lawful reason',['Never fire any employee','Fire workers for their race','Fire workers for reporting fraud'],'D','Exceptions include discrimination, retaliation and breach of contract.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'Which law bans firing or refusing to hire a woman because she is pregnant?','Pregnancy Discrimination Act',['Equal Pay Act of 1963','Fair Labor Standards Act','Hill-Burton Act of 1946'],'A','The law amended Title VII to treat pregnancy discrimination as sex discrimination.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'A patient believes their record contains an error. What right does HIPAA give them?','To request an amendment',['To delete the whole record','To sue without notice','To edit it themselves'],'B','If the provider denies the request, the patient may add a statement of disagreement.',RIT),
  q(BIO,'Which 1984 law makes it illegal to buy or sell human organs for transplant in the U.S.?','National Organ Transplant Act',['Uniform Anatomical Gift Act','Patient Self-Determination Act','Genetic Information Act'],'C','Living donors may still be repaid for reasonable expenses such as travel.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Which rule says vital organs may be taken only from donors who are already dead?','Dead donor rule',['Double effect rule','Minimum necessary rule','Two-challenge rule'],'D','The rule protects trust that organ recovery will never cause a patient’s death.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Organ recovery after the heart has permanently stopped is called donation after:','Circulatory death',['Brain growth','Full recovery','Surgical repair'],'A','Families usually decide to withdraw life support before any donation is discussed.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'What is a key ethical concern for a living kidney donor?','Risk to a healthy person',['The donor gains a new organ','The donor is always paid','The donor loses their vision'],'B','Donors must give free, informed consent without pressure from family.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Which 1927 Supreme Court case allowed forced sterilization under eugenics laws?','Buck v. Bell',['Roe v. Wade','Dobbs v. Jackson','Cruzan v. Missouri'],'C','The case is now widely viewed as a serious violation of human rights.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Genetic counselors who give information but avoid telling families what to choose are being:','Nondirective',['Paternalistic','Coercive','Deceptive'],'D','This respects the family’s autonomy in making reproductive decisions.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Testing all newborns for conditions such as PKU is an example of:','Newborn screening',['Gene therapy','Clinical research','Cloning'],'A','Early detection allows treatment that can prevent serious disability.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Combining DNA from three people to avoid passing on mitochondrial disease raises concerns about:','Changes passed to future generations',['Organ donor shortages','Workplace safety','Insurance billing errors in clinics'],'B','Mitochondrial replacement alters DNA that the child may pass on.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Which 1980s federal rules addressed withholding treatment from disabled newborns?','Baby Doe rules',['Stark rules','HIPAA rules','Belmont rules'],'C','They treated withholding care because of disability as a form of neglect.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'A physician withholds bad news, claiming it would seriously harm the patient. What is this exception called?','Therapeutic privilege',['Implied emergency consent','Qui tam action','Informed refusal'],'D','It is rarely justified and must not be used just to avoid hard conversations.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Why is giving a placebo while telling a patient it is real medicine ethically troubling?','It deceives the patient',['It always costs more','It is never effective','It is illegal in research'],'A','Honesty and informed consent are central to the patient-provider relationship.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Why do some critics object to rationing care using quality-adjusted life years (QALYs)?','It may undervalue people with disabilities',['It ignores all costs completely','It favors only the oldest patients','It is required by federal HIPAA privacy rules'],'B','Valuing life by quality scores can disadvantage people with chronic conditions.',TAM)
